WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.420
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Welcome to "Get

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:04.690
Cooking in Cloud," where
we share the best recipes

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:07.240
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.520
I'm Priyanka Vergadia.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.860
And in this episode, we will
talk about creating websites

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:12.910
on Google Cloud.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:15.385
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:26.110
If your business revenue
and customer satisfaction

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:30.010
depends on the availability and
scalability of your website,

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:31.900
then you are at the right place.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:36.040
In this episode, we will present
four reference architectures

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:41.060
that let you scale small or go
large depending on your needs.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.570
So let's dive in.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.250
At a high level, there
are four different recipes

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:46.480
to build websites.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.640
Depending on where
you are in your cloud

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.250
journey, your business
needs, and the maturity

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:54.160
of the development and
infrastructure teams,

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.570
one of these will
fit your needs.

00:00:56.570 --> 00:00:57.820
Let's see how.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:00:59.980
Static websites
are a good option

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.560
for sites like blogs,
where the page rarely

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.959
changes after it
has been published,

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:09.170
or where there isn't any
dynamically generated content.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:12.280
All you need to set up a
static website on Google Cloud

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:15.860
is Google Cloud Storage bucket,
connect it to your domain name,

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:16.660
and that's it.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.160
Pretty easy, right?

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.190
Now, as your business
starts to mature

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:23.800
and customers are
interested in buying things

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.600
from your website,
you might need

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.660
to generate dynamic content
and maybe enable payments.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.290
But if your company
is still small,

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:35.140
maybe you want to be
able to grow your website

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.780
without worrying
about scaling it based

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:39.580
on the increase in demand.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:42.070
In such a scenario,
we recommend that you

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:45.670
use Google Cloud's managed
and serverless offerings

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:49.120
like App Engine or
Cloud Run so you

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.880
can focus on delivering
features and let

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.660
Google worry about operating
and managing the infrastructure.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.790
This recipe provides
a wide range

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:01.540
of features that make
scalability, load balancing,

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:05.020
logging, monitoring,
and security much easier

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:08.470
than if you had to build and
manage the website yourself.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:10.780
For example, when
you use Cloud Run,

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:13.390
you can code in any
programming language,

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:16.960
because your application
is deployed as a container,

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.690
and Google will seamlessly
launch and scale

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:21.310
your application for you.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:23.330
So give it a try.

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:25.660
Now, for websites with
higher complexity,

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.030
you probably want more
options and control

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:30.100
than a managed platform offers.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.460
Whether it's configuring your
servers or virtual machines,

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:38.140
or if it's a need for a
specific memory, SSDs, and GPUs,

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.240
we recommend this third recipe--

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:41.740
Compute Engine.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:45.490
Compute Engine provides a
robust computing infrastructure,

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:47.680
but you must choose
and configure

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.710
the platform components
that you want to use.

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:54.130
Well, Google ensures that
resources are available,

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:58.660
reliable, and easy to use, but
it's up to you to provision

00:02:58.660 --> 00:02:59.920
and manage them.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.840
The advantage here
is that you have

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.880
complete control of the systems
and unlimited flexibility.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.690
The easiest way to deploy a
complete web-serving stack

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:15.070
on Compute Engine is by using
Google Cloud Marketplace.

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.650
With just a few
clicks, you can deploy

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:21.190
any of the over 100
fully realized solutions.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.410
For a more detailed
explanation on how

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:26.380
to setup Compute Engine to
serve scalable and resilient

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.560
websites, stay tuned
for upcoming episodes.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.460
Finally, for a larger business
with more developers and more

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:36.130
complicated problems,
we recommend

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:40.360
you use this fourth recipe and
containerize your applications.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.960
You will notice that it becomes
really hard to manage feature

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:47.530
rollouts if the website
is one big monolith, which

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:50.890
makes it difficult to keep up
with the increase in demand

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:52.390
and pace of business.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:56.960
Containerizing web applications
provides four key advantages.

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.380
First is
[? compartmentalization. ?]

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:02.440
As your apps design
becomes more complex,

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:06.590
containers are a good fit for
service-oriented architecture,

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:08.380
including microservices.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.200
This supports scalability.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.060
Second is portability.

00:04:13.060 --> 00:04:15.970
A container has everything
it needs to run.

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:19.600
Your app and its dependencies
are bundled together.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.350
This facilitates the
portability and fixes the,

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:28.660
"it works on my machine" problem
that many developers have.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.030
The third is rapid deployment.

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:33.130
When it's time to
deploy, your system

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:36.220
is built from a set of
definitions and images

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:39.350
so the parts can be
deployed quickly, reliably,

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:40.720
and automatically.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.760
Compared to virtual
machines, containers

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:46.330
are typically smaller
and deploy much quicker.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:49.900
Using Container to
deploy web apps on GKE

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.350
has further advantages
because one,

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:56.650
container orchestration
is built in, two,

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:59.050
Google Cloud offers
Container Registry, which

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:02.980
is a private storage for
container images, and three,

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:06.160
you can easily use the other
components of Google Cloud

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:08.320
Platform in your architecture.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.720
Using this
container-based recipe

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.510
makes sense if you have
a complicated website

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:16.300
with multiple different
moving pieces which

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:20.350
are better off in separate
services for easy management.

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:23.210
That's all for today on
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.180
Whether you're a
small blogger looking

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:29.560
to grow your community or a
huge multiscale e-commerce site,

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.380
hopefully, you've cooked
up something great.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.060
Join us next time,
because we will

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:37.900
share the recipe to host a
static website on Google Cloud.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:40.060
If you would like to
see more such content,

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:42.640
don't forget to like and
subscribe to our channel.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:45.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

